WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.836 --> 00:00:06.014
This is a machining centre: a CNC milling machine that changes its own tools.

2
00:00:06.748 --> 00:00:10.800
At the side hangs the magazine, with dozens of cutters and drills.

3
00:00:11.483 --> 00:00:16.603
The program calls one up, and the changer puts it in the spindle in a few seconds.

4
00:00:17.632 --> 00:00:20.941
In milling the tool spins, and the part is held still.

5
00:00:21.653 --> 00:00:27.493
Here a block of aluminium, 100 by 80 by 25 millimetres, sits in a vice.

6
00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:31.823
Usually the table moves left and right and back and forth,

7
00:00:32.091 --> 00:00:34.123
and the spindle moves up and down.

8
00:00:35.504 --> 00:00:40.810
The first tool, T1, is the face mill: a wide disc with inserts all around it.

9
00:00:41.856 --> 00:00:45.223
In two passes it makes the top flat, in 1 minute.

10
00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:48.729
The sizes still to come count from that face.

11
00:00:50.101 --> 00:00:54.281
Then T2, a end mill of 10 millimetres: a slim carbide rod,

12
00:00:54.408 --> 00:00:57.055
with cutting edges on its side and its tip.

13
00:00:58.108 --> 00:01:02.613
It mills a pocket of 60 by 40 millimetres, 8 millimetres deep.

14
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:06.425
That is a recess with a floor, like a small tray.

15
00:01:07.386 --> 00:01:12.309
T3, an 8 millimetre cutter, runs its side along the shape: the contour.

16
00:01:13.047 --> 00:01:15.984
A round cutter never makes a sharp inside corner.

17
00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:21.432
The corner is at least as round as the cutter’s radius: here 4 millimetres.

18
00:01:22.762 --> 00:01:27.127
Now a steel block, with an end mill of 12 millimetres and 3 teeth.

19
00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:32.416
A common start for carbide in steel is 150 metres per minute.

20
00:01:33.153 --> 00:01:34.372
Just as in turning,

21
00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:40.502
the diameter then gives the spindle speed: 3,980 revolutions a minute.

22
00:01:41.552 --> 00:01:44.153
Each tooth takes one bite per revolution.

23
00:01:44.888 --> 00:01:50.345
How thick it is, is the feed per tooth: here 0.05 millimetres.

24
00:01:51.321 --> 00:01:56.534
Feed per tooth, times the teeth, times the spindle speed: that is the table feed.

25
00:01:57.287 --> 00:02:01.989
Here 597 millimetres per minute, the F in the program.

26
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.090
The cutter cuts 10 millimetres deep,

27
00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:09.560
and sits 3 millimetres sideways in the material: the width of cut.

28
00:02:10.614 --> 00:02:16.756
Depth times width times table feed: 17.9 cubic centimetres come off a minute.

29
00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:21.915
That is the metal removal rate, which lets you compare two approaches fairly.

30
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:24.271
Watch one tooth.

31
00:02:24.996 --> 00:02:28.943
In climb milling it enters the material thick, and leaves it thin.

32
00:02:29.976 --> 00:02:33.807
In conventional milling it starts at zero, and rubs first.

33
00:02:34.556 --> 00:02:36.042
That makes heat and wear.

34
00:02:36.733 --> 00:02:40.610
So on a modern CNC machine, climb milling is the standard.

35
00:02:41.335 --> 00:02:45.062
Only on an old machine with backlash do you choose conventional,

36
00:02:45.189 --> 00:02:47.511
because climb can pull the table forward.

37
00:02:48.878 --> 00:02:52.616
If a 10 millimetre cutter sits only 1 millimetre sideways,

38
00:02:52.826 --> 00:02:57.807
the chip gets thinner than the feed per tooth: only 0.6 times as thick.

39
00:02:58.552 --> 00:02:59.725
That is chip thinning.

40
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.747
The cutter then rubs, instead of cutting.

41
00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:09.501
So you raise the feed per tooth, from 0.05 to 0.083.

42
00:03:10.144 --> 00:03:15.310
The table feed goes from 1,200 to 2,000 millimetres per minute,

43
00:03:15.473 --> 00:03:17.679
and the cutter actually lasts longer.

44
00:03:18.673 --> 00:03:21.866
That is what trochoidal milling builds on: small loops,

45
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.836
only about 5 to 15 percent of the diameter sideways,

46
00:03:25.975 --> 00:03:28.216
along almost the whole cutting length.

47
00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.926
Heat and force stay low, at a much higher feed.

48
00:03:33.268 --> 00:03:36.588
A five-axis machine has two more rotary axes.

49
00:03:37.318 --> 00:03:41.254
Here the part sits on a trunnion table, which tilts and turns.

50
00:03:41.974 --> 00:03:45.294
That way the cutter reaches five sides in one setup.

51
00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:51.896
In 3+2 machining it tilts the part to a fixed angle, locks the rotary axes,

52
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.578
and simply mills with X, Y and Z.

53
00:03:55.347 --> 00:03:57.065
That keeps the machine stiff.

54
00:03:58.099 --> 00:03:59.585
From 4 setups to 1.

55
00:04:00.284 --> 00:04:05.892
Each setup that goes saves about 30 minutes of changeover: 90 minutes per batch.

56
00:04:06.915 --> 00:04:11.013
In simultaneous 5-axis machining, all axes move at once.

57
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.113
That keeps the cutter at a steady angle to the domed face,

58
00:04:15.299 --> 00:04:17.191
as on the vanes of a pump impeller.
